imates\" vs previous iterations, and scale factor changed from likely ~0.25 to 0.325\n   - Fix: Revert to previous pricing methodology or obtain actual distributor quotes for major components\n\n**3. (severity HIGH) — Section 6 (Bill of Materials)**\n   - Finding: Several components are massively overpriced, particularly the DC-DC converter at £113.20 and Wi-Fi antenna at £113.20.\n   - Evidence: \"24V to 3.3V DC-DC converter, Traco Power, THN 15-2433WI, ~ £113.20\" and \"Wi-Fi antenna, Molex, 146153-0100, ~ £113.20\"\n   - Root cause: Engine B pricing curves incorrectly classifying small components as \"oem subsystem\" with £600 reference price\n   - Fix: Correct component classification - DC-DC converter should be ~£15-25, Wi-Fi antenna ~£2-5\n\n**4. (severity MED) — Section 2 (Module 4)**\n   - Finding: The 140mm centrifugal fan (80W) is severely undersized for an 8kW heat pump requiring 3000-4300 m³/h airflow.\n   - Evidence: \"The G2E140 blower can only deliver <500 m³/h, which is mathematically insufficient and will cause the evaporator to freeze rapidly\"\n   - Root cause: Module 2/6 specification error - wrong fan type and size\n   - Fix: Replace with 450-500mm axial fan rated for >3000 m³/h (e.g., ebm-papst HyBlade series)\n\n**5. (severity MED) — Section 6 (Bill of Materials)**\n   - Finding: The £1,800 target appears unachievable for an 8kW R290 heat pump with current market pricing.\n   - Evidence: \"£440 per kW thermal installed — 27% below typical range (£600–£900 typical)\" and market reference showing \"30 kW units show clear economies vs small <10 kW domestics\"\n   - Root cause: Brief target based on larger commercial unit economics, not applicable to 8kW residential scale\n   - Fix: Revise target to £2,400-2,800 based on realistic 8kW residential heat pump economics\n\n**6. (severity LOW) — Section 6 (Bill of Materials)**\n   - Finding: Emergency stop button priced at £113.20 is incorrectly classified as \"oem subsystem\".\n   - Evidence: \"emerge